<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<odoo>

    <!-- THE TWO PRICING PARAMETERS, SHIPPED AS A CALL RATHER THAN AS RECORDS.
         The markup is a percentage added to the supplier cost (80 means a cost
         of $100 is quoted at $180). The labour charge is a flat amount in
         currency, per assembly, added after the markup.

         WHY NOT <record noupdate="1">, WHICH IS WHAT THIS USED TO BE. A
         config parameter written through Settings goes via set_param, and
         set_param given a falsy value DELETES the row - which is what Odoo
         hands it for a Float of zero. That deletion takes the record's xmlid
         with it, and the next write recreates the row WITHOUT one. The module
         then believes its own record is missing and tries to INSERT it, which
         collides with the existing row on the unique key for `key` and the
         whole registry fails to load. Not a theoretical risk: it happened on
         this build, from nothing more exotic than setting a value to zero and
         then upgrading the module.

         A function cannot collide, because it addresses the parameter by key -
         which is its real identity - rather than by an xmlid that the ORM is
         free to drop. It runs on install and on every update, creates only what
         is absent, and so leaves an edited figure alone exactly as noupdate
         was meant to. -->
    <function model="ir.config_parameter" name="_spring_engineering_ensure_defaults"/>

</odoo>
